1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.402
In this lesson, you're going to take the insurance

2
00:00:02.402 --> 00:00:04.771
policy agent that you've built in the previous lesson

3
00:00:04.871 --> 00:00:06.873
and make it support the A2A protocol.

4
00:00:07.173 --> 00:00:09.342
You'll use the A2A Python SDK, which

5
00:00:09.342 --> 00:00:11.478
handles a lot of the low-level details,

6
00:00:11.778 --> 00:00:13.646
so you'll just need to focus on

7
00:00:13.646 --> 00:00:15.782
what happens when the agent receives a request.

8
00:00:15.982 --> 00:00:18.985
Let's get coding. Here is the code of the server agent

9
00:00:19.185 --> 00:00:23.256
that I'll save as a Python file, a2a_policy_agent.py.

10
00:00:23.256 --> 00:00:27.060
For that, I'm again using the write file Jupyter magic function,

11
00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:29.162
which will not run this cell directly,

12
00:00:29.462 --> 00:00:31.865
but will copy its content to the Python file.

13
00:00:32.065 --> 00:00:35.068
First, I'll import all the libraries I'll be using,

14
00:00:35.468 --> 00:00:38.471
including the modules from the A2A Python SDK

15
00:00:38.772 --> 00:00:41.274
and the PolicyAgent class I just created.

16
00:00:41.775 --> 00:00:43.777
I'm going to wrap up the PolicyAgent

17
00:00:44.077 --> 00:00:46.079
in what's called an AgentExecutor.

18
00:00:46.546 --> 00:00:48.348
This is a class that you

19
00:00:48.348 --> 00:00:50.417
write which inherits from the AgentExecutor class

20
00:00:50.417 --> 00:00:52.252
in the A2A Python SDK.

21
00:00:52.819 --> 00:00:55.455
In this case, I'll call it PolicyAgentExecutor.

22
00:00:56.022 --> 00:00:58.758
This is what links the generic A2A protocol plumbing

23
00:00:58.758 --> 00:01:00.560
handled by the A2A SDK

24
00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:02.529
and the specific logic of your agent.

25
00:01:03.063 --> 00:01:04.931
I'll add a __init__ method.

26
00:01:05.331 --> 00:01:08.334
All this will do is initialize the PolicyAgent class

27
00:01:08.535 --> 00:01:11.337
and save the instance as self.agent.

28
00:01:11.638 --> 00:01:13.740
Next, I'll create an execute method.

29
00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:16.342
This will take in the request context,

30
00:01:16.342 --> 00:01:19.612
which includes the user input and an event queue,

31
00:01:19.712 --> 00:01:21.381
which is where the output will

32
00:01:21.381 --> 00:01:23.316
be sent and read by A2A clients.

33
00:01:23.616 --> 00:01:25.718
To get the user prompt, you just call

34
00:01:25.718 --> 00:01:28.321
context.get_user_input

35
00:01:28.621 --> 00:01:30.490
In a production environment, you'd want to

36
00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:32.625
read in the context ID to manage state.

37
00:01:33.093 --> 00:01:35.328
But for simplicity, I'll skip that for

38
00:01:35.328 --> 00:01:37.530
now. Then you can send that prompt

39
00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:41.167
to the agent.answer_query method and get the response.

40
00:01:41.601 --> 00:01:44.003
In this case, it should be a pretty quick turnaround.

41
00:01:44.404 --> 00:01:48.308
So I'll just wrap up that text response as an A2A message.

42
00:01:48.775 --> 00:01:50.477
Then I'll send it to the event_queue

43
00:01:50.677 --> 00:01:53.213
with event_queue.enqueue_event

44
00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:56.783
and await that because it's an asynchronous method.

45
00:01:57.083 --> 00:02:01.087
The AgentExecutor class also requires you to implement the cancel method,

46
00:02:01.287 --> 00:02:03.389
but I don't need that because I'm

47
00:02:03.389 --> 00:02:05.792
just doing synchronous communication. So, I'll just pass.

48
00:02:06.092 --> 00:02:08.294
Now, I'll create the main method

49
00:02:08.495 --> 00:02:11.097
where I'll define all of the metadata for the agent.

50
00:02:11.297 --> 00:02:13.299
I'll first add a print statement

51
00:02:13.466 --> 00:02:15.568
to confirm that the agent is running,

52
00:02:15.768 --> 00:02:17.504
load our environment variables. Now, this agent

53
00:02:17.504 --> 00:02:19.472
is going to be run as a server,

54
00:02:19.572 --> 00:02:22.876
so you need a host and a port number.

55
00:02:23.376 --> 00:02:26.479
So I'll get these for the agent from the environment variables.

56
00:02:26.579 --> 00:02:30.183
In this course, You're going to use localhost for all the agents.

57
00:02:30.483 --> 00:02:33.086
But these can be running on multiple remote servers.

58
00:02:33.486 --> 00:02:35.488
Then, I'll define the AgentSkill.

59
00:02:35.688 --> 00:02:37.690
This is what the agent can do.

60
00:02:37.891 --> 00:02:40.393
In this case, I'll call it insurance coverage.

61
00:02:40.894 --> 00:02:45.198
The description is, provides information about insurance coverage options and details.

62
00:02:45.398 --> 00:02:47.867
I'll add some tags and example queries.

63
00:02:48.368 --> 00:02:50.870
Now, I'll define the AgentCard.

64
00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:53.573
This is that digital business card for the agent.

65
00:02:53.573 --> 00:02:56.376
The name will be InsurancePolicyCoverageAgent.

66
00:02:56.876 --> 00:02:59.579
I'll pass in the URL for where the agent will be hosted,

67
00:02:59.579 --> 00:03:01.581
and a version number for the agent,

68
00:03:01.581 --> 00:03:04.884
define the input and output modes as text only.

69
00:03:04.884 --> 00:03:07.187
We aren't supporting streaming for this agent.

70
00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:10.223
So I'll mark streaming as false in capabilities,

71
00:03:10.924 --> 00:03:13.026
and I'll pass in that skill that I created.

72
00:03:13.426 --> 00:03:15.929
Now, I'll create a request handler and server,

73
00:03:16.229 --> 00:03:18.731
which is an Starlette Fast API application.

74
00:03:19.232 --> 00:03:20.833
This will be hosting the agent card

75
00:03:21.034 --> 00:03:23.136
and direct requests to the agent executor.

76
00:03:23.536 --> 00:03:25.605
Then, I'll run the server using uvicorn

77
00:03:25.805 --> 00:03:27.607
with the host and port.

78
00:03:28.508 --> 00:03:31.077
And I'll end the script with the dunder main block.

79
00:03:31.578 --> 00:03:34.080
So now our agent is saved as a Python file.

80
00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:36.382
So you can run the server in a terminal.

81
00:03:36.683 --> 00:03:39.085
I'll use this block to display the terminal in the notebook

82
00:03:39.285 --> 00:03:41.287
so that you can run the server and keep it going,

83
00:03:41.487 --> 00:03:43.590
because you'll need to interact with this agent later.

84
00:03:43.990 --> 00:03:45.892
I'll be using different terminals for each agent.

85
00:03:45.992 --> 00:03:48.494
For the policy agent, I'm using terminal one.

86
00:03:48.928 --> 00:03:52.131
You can run the agent with uv run

87
00:03:52.131 --> 00:03:54.734
a2a_policy_agent.

88
00:03:55.935 --> 00:03:57.737
And you can see that it's running.

89
00:03:58.338 --> 00:04:00.139
Notice, nothing else is happening yet.

90
00:04:00.740 --> 00:04:02.742
That's because I've just started the server.

91
00:04:02.742 --> 00:04:04.744
We haven't sent any requests to the agent yet.

92
00:04:04.744 --> 00:04:06.312
Be sure to keep this agent running

93
00:04:06.312 --> 00:04:08.147
because we'll need it in the next lesson.

94
00:04:08.448 --> 00:04:12.151
Speaking of which, in the next lesson, you'll create an A2A client

95
00:04:12.151 --> 00:04:14.320
which can connect to this A2A server.
